# cww services env — the pass-through for env your app or its tooling needs at
# runtime. Everything here is loaded verbatim into the workspace container
# (compose env_file). Mode 600.
#
# Three host-side layers, read at 'cww create' time. Copy this file to
# whichever one matches the scope of the value:
#
#   ~/.cww/services.env                        every workspace on this machine
#   ~/.cww/services/<project>.env              every workspace of this project
#   ~/.cww/services/<project>/<workspace>.env  just this workspace
#
# A more specific layer overrides a broader one, so a machine-wide default can
# be re-pointed for one project, or for one workspace. <project> and
# <workspace> are lowercased with non-alphanumerics turned into dashes — and
# 'cww create' prints all three resolved paths, so you never have to guess them.
#
# These live on the host, never in the repo, so they may hold secrets. For
# values you want to share with the team, prefer 'environment:' entries in the
# repo's .cww/docker-compose.services.yml, which rides the repo.
#
# Values are read when the container is created: after editing any of these,
# run 'cww teardown <workspace>' then 'cww create <workspace>' to pick them up.
# The files themselves survive a teardown.
#
# Agent credentials do NOT belong here — they live in ~/.cww/env, and each
# workspace receives only the key of the auth method it was created with.
# Git credentials live in ~/.cww/credentials (see examples/cww.env.example).
# Both are injected after these layers, so nothing here can shadow them.
#
# Examples:
# NPM_CONFIG_REGISTRY=https://registry.example.internal
# SENTRY_DSN=...
